{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NoncommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure.rotate_ijk_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure.rotate_ijk_sq","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BringsSurface/ColourComplexStructure.lean#L51-L62","doc":"**Rotation step — anti-commuting complex structures give the quaternion `k`.**\nIn any (associative) ring, if `i, j` are complex structures (`i² = j² = -1`) that\nanti-commute (`j·i = -(i·j)`), then `k := i·j` is again a complex structure:\n`k² = -1`. This is the \"rotate across `i, j, k`\" that builds `ℍ`. The proof uses\nassociativity — the reason the intuition stops at `ℍ` and not `𝕆`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure.complex_mul_from_sqrt_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BringsSurface.ColourComplexStructure.complex_mul_from_sqrt_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":42,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BringsSurface/ColourComplexStructure.lean#L42-L49","doc":"**Colour atom — complex multiplication from an imaginary unit.**\nIn any commutative ring, an element `j` with `j² = -1` makes `a + b·j` multiply\nexactly like complex numbers. This single complex structure is the colour `ℂ`;\nthe colour triplet `ℂ³` is three such directions, on which `SU(3)` acts. ","signatureChunk":0}]}